  {
    "title": "2) Learning-based data fusion",
    "subtitle": "",
    "note": "We use AMG-Fuse’s MSAKNet, loading the authors’ Haze checkpoint, already trained for visible–infrared fusion in haze. We do not start from random weights and perform no fine-tuning of our own in this second method. The network contains convolutions for local structures and Transformer blocks with attention to relate features. The visible, thermal and fusion branches exchange information at multiple scales. Features are learned numerical maps, not pedestrian labels. To run on the CPU, we reduce both inputs to 40 × 32 pixels, extract features before the final output_f convolution and apply this original layer to obtain a small RGB reconstruction. The next slide explains how this reconstruction guides fusion of the 320 × 256 inputs. This is an AMG-Fuse-guided variant, distinct from its native full-resolution output.",
    "layout": "neural-fusion",
    "elements": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
