  {
    "title": "How the network guides fusion",
    "subtitle": "The code takes R, then computes α by comparing it with the two inputs.",
    "note": "The network does not directly produce the alpha map used in our final blend. First, output_f reconstructs a result R at 40 × 32 pixels. At each position, we compute the mean squared distance across the three channels between R and the reduced visible input, dV, and between R and the reduced thermal input, dT. The thermal input is expanded to three channels. We define alpha = sigmoid(4 × (dT − dV)). If R is closer to the visible input, dV is smaller than dT and alpha exceeds 0.5; if it is closer to the thermal input, alpha is below 0.5. The sigmoid keeps coefficients between zero and one. We enlarge the map to 320 × 256 with bilinear interpolation and compute F = alpha V + (1 − alpha) T, using the higher-resolution inputs to retain detail. Fixed averaging corresponds to alpha always equal to 0.5; here alpha depends on the inputs and varies by position. For example, alpha = 0.8 means 80% visible and 20% thermal: this illustrates its interpretation and is not a measured value. Alpha is not a reliability probability. Any detection benefit must be measured with YOLO. This procedure is our AMG-Fuse-guided variant, not its native full-resolution output.",
    "layout": "neural-fusion-weights",
    "elements": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "x": 0.75,
        "y": 0.9,
        "w": 11.8,
        "h": 0.85,
        "text": "How the network guides fusion",
        "size": 31,
        "color": "#183249",
        "bold": true,
        "align": "left"
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "x": 0.75,
        "y": 1.85,
        "w": 11.8,
        "h": 0.6,
        "text": "The code takes R, then computes α by comparing it with the two inputs.",
        "size": 17,
        "color": "#51677b",
        "bold": false,
        "align": "left"
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "x": 0.75,
        "y": 7.05,
